## 1. Organize Your Fitness App Repository

A clear repository structure helps developers understand where application code, documentation, tests, and configuration files belong.

The following is an illustrative structure for a fitness application with mobile and backend components.

```text
fitness-app/
│
├── README.md
├── LICENSE
├── CONTRIBUTING.md
├── SECURITY.md
├── CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
├── .gitignore
│
├── assets/
│   └── fitness-app-repository-setup.png
│
├── src/
│   ├── mobile/
│   │   ├── workouts/
│   │   ├── nutrition/
│   │   ├── authentication/
│   │   └── integrations/
│   │
│   └── backend/
│       ├── api/
│       ├── services/
│       ├── models/
│       └── middleware/
│
├── tests/
│   ├── unit/
│   ├── integration/
│   └── e2e/
│
├── docs/
│   ├── ARCHITECTURE.md
│   └── OWNERSHIP.md
│
└── .github/
    ├── CODEOWNERS
    ├── ISSUE_TEMPLATE/
    ├── PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md
    └── workflows/
```

This layout separates application functionality from repository management.

Actual directory names should reflect the project's technology stack. A Flutter application, React Native project, or native iOS and Android repository may require a different structure.

### Essential Repository Files

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| README.md | Project introduction and setup instructions |
| LICENSE | Software licensing terms |
| CONTRIBUTING.md | Development and contribution guidelines |
| SECURITY.md | Vulnerability reporting procedures |
| CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md | Community participation standards |
| CODEOWNERS | Code review responsibilities |
| .gitignore | Excludes local secrets and generated files |

Not every private repository requires every optional governance document. Choose files based on project complexity, collaboration needs, and security risks.

