## 9. Set Up Testing and Continuous Integration

Automated checks help identify defects before changes reach protected branches.

A fitness app repository should consider the following checks:

| Test type | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| Linting | Detect code-quality and formatting problems |
| Unit tests | Verify individual calculations and functions |
| Integration tests | Validate connected services and data flows |
| Build checks | Confirm supported applications compile |
| Dependency scanning | Identify known dependency vulnerabilities |
| Secret scanning | Detect accidentally committed credentials |

The exact CI configuration depends on the application's technology stack.

A native Swift project, Kotlin application, Flutter app, and React Native repository require different build and test commands.

Avoid copying a generic CI workflow without confirming that its commands and dependencies match the project.

### Recommended CI Workflow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Developer Opens Pull Request] --> B[Run Lint and Static Checks]
    B --> C[Run Unit Tests]
    C --> D[Run Integration Tests]
    D --> E[Run Security and Dependency Checks]
    E --> F{All Required Checks Pass?}
    F -->|Yes| G[Code Owner Review]
    F -->|No| H[Fix Issues]
    H --> B
    G --> I{Approved?}
    I -->|Yes| J[Merge Through Approved Process]
    I -->|No| H
```

Passing CI checks does not replace human code review, security assessment, or device-level testing.

For wearable-connected applications, physical-device validation may still be necessary.

