## 11. Repository Setup Checklist

Use this checklist before inviting developers or external contributors.

### Repository Structure

- [ ] README explains the fitness application.
- [ ] Directory structure is documented.
- [ ] Setup instructions are accurate.
- [ ] Tests and documentation are organized.

### Licensing and Ownership

- [ ] Software license or proprietary-use notice is defined.
- [ ] Third-party asset and dependency rights are reviewed.
- [ ] Repository maintainers are identified.
- [ ] Intellectual property responsibilities are documented.
- [ ] Contractor and employee contributions follow applicable agreements.

### Collaboration

- [ ] CONTRIBUTING.md is available.
- [ ] Pull request standards are documented.
- [ ] CODEOWNERS references valid accounts.
- [ ] Appropriate branch protections or rulesets are configured.
- [ ] Issue and security reporting processes are established.

### Quality and Security

- [ ] Automated tests are configured where appropriate.
- [ ] No production credentials are committed.
- [ ] Test fixtures contain no real sensitive health information.
- [ ] Workout and nutrition validation rules are tested.
- [ ] Dependencies and external integrations are reviewed.
- [ ] Security findings have a documented escalation path.

This checklist should be adapted to the project's size, technology, distribution model, and risk profile.

---

## Conclusion

A well-maintained GitHub repository supports more than source-code storage.

Clear licensing helps define how software may be used. Documented ownership separates legal rights from maintenance responsibilities. Contributor guidelines create a consistent development workflow, while code reviews and automated testing help protect application quality.

For fitness applications, these practices are particularly valuable when teams manage workout data, nutrition calculations, wearable integrations, subscriptions, and personal information.

**The objective is to make every contribution understandable, reviewable, secure, and maintainable—not to introduce unnecessary repository bureaucracy.**

Start with clear documentation, appropriate licensing, responsible access controls, and a contribution process that fits the project. Improve those practices as the development team and application grow.
